<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José María Córdova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José María Córdova was a general of the wars of independence from Antioquia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>. He distinguished himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a> and especially at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> in 1824, where he led the decisive attack. Opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and the plans for a monarchy, he rose in revolt in Antioquia in 1829 and was killed after his defeat at El Santuario.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-maria-cordova.jpg" alt="José María Córdova" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José María Espinosa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Mar%C3%ADa_C%C3%B3rdova._An%C3%B3nimo,_ca._1840._Colecci%C3%B3n_Museo_Nacional_de_Colombia._Reg._350.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José María Córdova Muñoz was born at Concepción in Antioquia on 8 September 1799, the son of Crisanto de Córdova y Mesa, a small merchant, and Pascuala Muñoz Castrillón. He had only a basic schooling and travelled with his father on business. In June 1814 he was admitted as a cadet to the military school that Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jose-de-caldas">Francisco José de Caldas</a> had set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a>, where the French veteran Emanuel Roergas Serviez became his mentor and later made him his aide-de-camp.</p><p>At the Battle of the Palo River on 5 July 1815 he took part in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> charge, was knocked down by a bullet through his hat and was wrongly reported dead. He was promoted lieutenant at the age of 16.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the patriot defeat of 1816 he retreated with Serviez to the Llanos of Casanare and fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> at El Yagual. He blamed Páez for Serviez's assassination, tried to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s army, and was court-martialled for desertion, escaping execution through the intervention of Pedro Camejo. After the Battle of Mucuritas in April 1817 he joined Bolívar and was made captain at Angostura that November.</p><p>In the campaign of 1819 he fought at Gámeza and the Vargas Swamp before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyaca-department">Boyacá</a>, then at 19 was sent with 50 men to free Antioquia; his volunteers defeated Colonel Francisco Warleta at Chorros Blancos on 12 February 1820. He fought at Cartagena in 1821 and led the charge of the Magdalena battalion that broke the royalist line before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> on 24 May 1822. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824 his 2nd Colombian Division routed the Monet Division, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> promoted him General of Division on the field, placing his own epaulettes on his shoulders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Córdova commanded the new armed forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> until 1827. Back in Antioquia, he was under investigation over the conspiracy of 25 September 1828 against Bolívar. Bolívar sent the Irish general Daniel Florence O'Leary against his revolt, and near El Santuario Córdova was killed by the Irish commander Rupert Hand on 17 October 1829. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Córdova, the Colombian army's officers' academy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a> international airport at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rionegro">Rionegro</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José María Córdova” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reports that Bolívar’s ministers were sounding out European courts about a monarchy to succeed him angered liberals, and in 1829 General <b>José María Córdova</b> rose in revolt in Antioquia and was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Concepción, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. Died in El Santuario, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.4,-75.26&amp;year=1818" aria-label="Concepción on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2466 1925 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José María Córdova"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2916 2195h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2916 2195h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.4,-75.26&amp;year=1818">What was here in 1818? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
